  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Gage Jump shut down Boston over six scoreless innings, and the offense waited until the seventh to finally break through.",
    "what_this_means": "Jump's shutout was the game's foundation. Three hits and seven strikeouts over six innings meant the Athletics could afford to be patient. They trailed no one. They just hadn't scored. Then in the seventh, two runs arrived, enough to hold off Boston's late push in the ninth. Tommy White went 4-for-4, Henry Bolte drove in a run. The bullpen gave back two. Luis Medina allowed one in 1.2 innings, Hogan Harris surrendered two in 1.1 but closed it out. This is what happens when a starter gives you air to breathe: you don't have to be urgent, just efficient when your moment comes. The Athletics were neither early nor late. They were just late enough to matter.",
    "what_to_watch": "Boston tonight brings Patrick Sandoval, whose 3.21 ERA has been one of the AL West's steadiest offerings, and the Athletics counter with Jacob Lopez on a night when the pitching advantage sits with the visitor.",
